<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lithuanian-Byelorussian SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1919 CE · capital Vilnius</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Litbel, a short-lived Soviet republic joining Lithuania and Belarus in 1919, created as a buffer against Poland and soon overrun.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In February 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> merged the Soviet republics of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussia</a>, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/socialist-soviet-republic-of-byelorussia">Socialist Soviet Republic of Byelorussia</a>, into a single Lithuanian-Byelorussian Soviet Socialist Republic, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-byelorussian-soviet-socialist-republic">Litbel</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincas-mickevicius-kapsukas">Vincas Mickevicius-Kapsukas</a> chaired its council of people's commissars.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-byelorussian-soviet-socialist-republic">Litbel</a> was a Bolshevik state with nationalised land and industry, governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party</a> and backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. It was intended as a buffer between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Soviet Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, but it had little popular support among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> and controlled only part of the territory it claimed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Polish army captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> in April 1919 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in August, and the Lithuanian army pushed from the west. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-byelorussian-soviet-socialist-republic">Litbel</a> effectively ceased to exist by the summer of 1919. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a> was re-created in 1920.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lithuanian-byelorussian-ssr.jpg" alt="Lithuanian-Byelorussian SSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Lithuanian-Byelorussian_SSR.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vilnius</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vincas Mickevicius-Kapsukas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>State atheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian, Lithuanian, Belarusian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Lithuania, Belarus</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Litbel</dd></dl></aside></div>
